---
kind: quest
luteVersion: "0.21.0"
uses: ../world.schema.yaml
title: Hold the Spine
---

/*
  The one quest in this tree with NO `after=`, and the omission is DELIBERATE — the
  corpus's only worked example of what the connectivity layer does with a bare quest,
  so a reader who "fixes" it deletes the evidence (see T4.7, and the "T4 controller
  decision" that ruled this file is not retrofitted while Task 9's five siblings all
  carry `after=`). See the README's "Deliberate imperfections".

  What it costs, verified with `lute scenario docs/examples/haven`: this quest appears
  in NO topological layer and on NO edge — `after=` is the ONLY thing that puts a
  quest into the prerequisite graph, and no amount of `start=`/`done=` work will
  place it. Since dsl 0.21.0 (§7a.5) it is no longer silently absent: the report
  lists it after the edges, under `unanchored (no `after` — available from the start
  of play; no prerequisites in this graph):`, and `scenario reach quest:holdTheSpine`
  gives it its own verdict, `Unanchored — a quest with no declared `after`
  prerequisite: available from the start of play; …`. The graph still holds five
  quests, not six. `scenario envelope quest:holdTheSpine` still degrades to the
  defaults-only `D` table (dsl §4.4) plus a note offering the full project-resolved
  envelope if `after` were declared.

  Story-wise that is also true: the coupling can be held from the moment somebody who
  can halt the shed is awake, which `start="holds(can_halt(toma))"` already says. The
  gate is the fact, not the route. `after=` would add a route prerequisite the story
  does not have — so the honest declaration is the one that costs graph membership.
*/
<quest id="holdTheSpine" title="Hold the Spine" start="holds(can_halt(toma))">
<objective id="reachToma" title="Reach the spine coupling" done="run.shedPressure >= 1"/>
<on event="questComplete">
::set{run.vesnaTrust += 1}
@narrator: The shed halted, one module short of the infirmary.
</on>
</quest>
